{quote}should I refund total order? we actually can not do partial refunds at the moment.. but the trip did happen. but also maybe refund them completely as a compensation for the hassle this has been. (Orders should be 5-10€ max are being charged between 80€-110€);{quote}

The refund decision is up to you. Fraud shouldn’t have any impact on that specific decision, because as I’ve said, we can’t really say with 100% confidence that it's fraud. But personally, yes, I would suggest doing a full refund on those overcharged orders, especially the clear ones where the driver goes to the requested destination and just drives more after boosting the price too much, like 15x-25x. 

{quote}(just to make sure I understood the strategy) you propose a real time re-calculator at the end of every business order by the system that understands if the order was over charged ? this would be great. just wanted to understand a little bit better how it would do that.{quote}

At the end of the order, Pricing uses an API that calls Fraud where we can make a decision to recalculate the order. atm, our options are limited, but one of them is to recalculate the order back to the upfront price. The condition we’ll put are: 

* the final price is at least 5 times bigger than the initial prediction;
* it’s an order with business as a payment method;
* there’s no logged destination change in the order.

If those conditions are met, the order gets recalculated back to the upfront automatically, so the overcharge doesn’t happen. In those examples, imagine the upfront is 5 euros, there are no changes, and the final price is 100. We’ll put it back at 5 automatically. If you open the order, you’ll see a lot of kms, a lot of time, but the final price will be 5 😄 

To go live with this, would appreciate [~accountid:60e96a31131bf80069e1459f] input here. Since it might affect the business. Logically, should be safe considering the conditions, and I provided historical data since Feb 1st, but that’s still a business decision. We won’t be re-calculating because we’re confident it’s fraud, that recalculation is based on fraud indicators only.

